Leaky Gut Syndrome (Increased Intestinal Permeability)
Integrative & Functional medicine
Chronic abdominal bloating, New-onset multiple food intolerances, Widespread migrating joint aching, Elevated serum zonulin

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IFM-020
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe exacerbation of intractable abdominal bloating, agonizing migrating joint pain, and profound lethargy requiring urgent assessment to rule out acute inflammatory bowel disease flare or acute mechanical bowel obstruction.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated a patient in severe distress with a grossly distended abdomen, severe migrating polyarthralgia, and inability to tolerate oral intake due to severe pain. Urgent on-site physical and abdominal assessment was strictly required to rule out acute bowel ischemia, toxic megacolon, or acute surgical peritonitis, preventing catastrophic gastrointestinal or systemic deterioration prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My stomach is so swollen I look nine months pregnant, and every joint in my body is throbbing with a deep, sickening pain."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a rapid, severe escalation of chronic, recurrent generalized abdominal bloating and profound flatulence over the past 24 hours. The patient currently exhibits highly irregular distinct bowel habits, predominantly severe loose stools today. Over the past few weeks, the patient has developed distinct new-onset severe food intolerances, noting that almost everything they eat now triggers bloating. Concurrently, the patient complains of widespread, vague, and distinct migrating joint and muscle aching indicating systemic inflammation, which tonight has become agonizing and prevents sleep. The patient also reports severe brain fog, profound generalized fatigue, and a distinct intense severe migraine. The patient notes an exacerbation of an underlying severe chronic skin condition (eczema) over the past three days.
Functional Status: Bedbound, highly distressed, unable to find a comfortable position due to combined abdominal and joint pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; unable to prepare meals or manage basic self-care due to severe pain and systemic lethargy.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 130/80 | HR: 95 | Temp: 37.2 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Patient appears chronically ill, acutely distressed by abdominal discomfort, frequently shifting position, and displaying visible patches of severe, excoriated eczema on the flexor surfaces.
Physical Exam: Abdominal exam reveals gross, profound generalized distension. The abdomen is tympanitic to percussion diffusely. Palpation reveals widespread, moderate tenderness but crucially lacks true rigidity, involuntary guarding, or rebound tenderness. Bowel sounds are hyperactive. Musculoskeletal exam reveals widespread, diffuse migrating joint tenderness (knees, wrists, shoulders) but entirely lacks focal, hot, erythematous monoarticular effusion. Neurological exam reveals an intact sensorium but obvious cognitive sluggishness. The clinical picture perfectly aligns with chronic abdominal bloating, new-onset food intolerances, and widespread migrating joint aching. Confirmation requires specialized functional urine testing showing an abnormal lactulose-to-mannitol recovery ratio or elevated serum zonulin.
Environmental Observations: Bedroom is heavily cluttered with various over-the-counter supplements and half-empty bottles of antacids.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient taking high-dose over-the-counter NSAIDs for joint pain (which may be exacerbating the intestinal permeability); no prescribed immunosuppressants.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Leaky Gut Syndrome (Increased Intestinal Permeability) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Toxic Megacolon (complicating undiagnosed IBD) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of high fever, profound tachycardia/shock, and lack of severe, bloody diarrhea with rigid peritonitis.

Acute Small Bowel Obstruction - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Passage of loose stools/flatus is maintained, bowel sounds are hyperactive (not silent or high-pitched tinkling), and no bilious vomiting.

Acute Septic Arthritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Pain is distinctly generalized and migratory, entirely lacking a single, grossly swollen, hot, and erythematous joint.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute, severe abdominal distension, migrating polyarthralgia, and significant distress. Telephone triage correctly identified the critical need to evaluate for a life-threatening acute surgical abdomen, such as toxic megacolon or a high-grade bowel obstruction. Immediate on-site clinical assessment was strictly necessary to directly palpate the abdomen for signs of peritonitis, auscultate bowel activity, and assess hemodynamic stability. The physical exclusion of a rigid, silent abdomen, high fever, and focal septic joints confirmed the absence of an immediate, life-threatening emergency. However, the severity of the painful distension, the agonizing migrating joint pain, and the functional inability to tolerate oral intake dictated that delaying assessment until the next in-hours period posed a distinct, unacceptable risk of severe dehydration, uncontrolled severe pain, and psychological collapse. This urgent intervention unequivocally satisfies MBS Items 585-600 criteria.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Advised strict cessation of all over-the-counter NSAIDs immediately, as they directly disrupt the gut mucosal barrier. Advised a temporary liquid-only diet (bone broth, clear fluids) for 24-48 hours to minimize digestive burden. Prescribed a short course of Paracetamol 1g QID PRN for joint analgesia. Recommend routine GP follow-up for specialized functional urine testing (lactulose/mannitol) and comprehensive stool analysis.
Positive Behavioral Change: To physically repair the mucosal barrier and reduce systemic inflammation, initiate specific dietary and supplemental interventions. Action: Consume one cup of high-quality, collagen-rich bone broth twice daily. Consequence: The specific amino acids (glutamine, glycine) in the broth physically provide the necessary building blocks for the intestinal lining to repair tight junctions, directly rewarding the patient with a reduction in both bloating and migrating joint pain. Action: Chew every mouthful of solid food a minimum of 30 times before swallowing. Consequence: Mechanical breakdown in the mouth significantly reduces the chemical and structural burden on the compromised small intestine, explicitly preventing the fermentation that causes severe bloating.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated currently.
DME/Safety (Optional): None indicated.
Follow-up: Routine GP review within 72 hours for specialist referral (Gastroenterology/Functional Medicine) and advanced testing.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department or call 000 if developing sudden, unbearable "knife-like" abdominal pain, vomiting blood, passing large amounts of bright red blood or black tarry stools, or developing a high fever with a rigid, board-like stomach.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending investigations; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: The acute bloating and joint pain will slowly improve over 3-5 days of strict bowel rest and removal of NSAIDs. Long-term mucosal repair takes months of dedicated dietary intervention.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate emergency care if your stomach becomes hard as a rock, if you start vomiting repeatedly and cannot keep water down, or if you pass dark, black blood in your stool.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The lining of your gut has become highly irritated and permeable, allowing undigested food particles to leak into your bloodstream. Your immune system attacks these particles, causing the massive bloating and the severe migrating joint pain. The NSAIDs you are taking are actually making the leak worse.
Actionable Steps: Stop taking Ibuprofen/NSAIDs immediately. Switch to a liquid diet of bone broth and water for the next 24 hours to let your gut physically rest and begin to heal. Use only Paracetamol for the joint pain.

